sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch09-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Smooth continuum behavior, such as diffusion of density, emerges from discrete systems whenever small-scale apparent randomness washes out individual details and conserved quantities constrain overall features, as shown by block cellular automata whose average density follows the diffusion equation.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Continuum behavior emerges from discrete randomizing conserving rules";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 9, “Fundamental Physics”; section “Conserved Quantities and Continuum Phenomena” (from p. 458), p. 464. Scope: discrete systems with conserved quantities and intrinsic randomness, such as particle-conserving block cellular automata and fluids"
}
